T5, J1. Maybe less useful.\n\nDiscarding W2: leaves W4, W7, T5, J1. Similar to above.\n\nDiscarding T5: leaves W2, W4, W7, J1. Maybe not helpful.\n\nDiscarding W7: leaves W2, W4, T5, J1. Also similar.\n\nSo the best option seems to be to discard J1, as it's a single and not part of any potential meld or pair. By discarding J1, I keep the other tiles which have more potential for forming a pair or meld in the future.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The best move is to discard J1 to maximize the potential for forming a winning hand by retaining key tiles for possible pairs or melds.\nReason: Discarding J1 (a single Dragon tile) allows keeping W2, W4, W7, and T5, which can potentially form a pair (e.g., T5 with another T5) or a meld (e.g., a Pung of W7 if drawn). This action preserves flexibility for future draws while reducing deadwood.\nAnswer: Play J1"}
